  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"151\" data-end=\"358\">The <strong data-start=\"155\" data-end=\"171\">Ammonia test</strong> measures the level of <strong data-start=\"194\" data-end=\"218\">ammonia in the blood</strong>, which is a byproduct of <strong data-start=\"244\" data-end=\"266\">protein metabolism</strong>. Normally, ammonia is converted to <strong data-start=\"302\" data-end=\"310\">urea</strong> in the <strong data-start=\"318\" data-end=\"327\">liver</strong> and excreted by the kidneys.</p>\n<p data-start=\"360\" data-end=\"393\">This test is primarily used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"394\" data-end=\"732\">\n<li data-start=\"394\" data-end=\"467\">\n<p data-start=\"396\" data-end=\"467\">Evaluate <strong data-start=\"405\" data-end=\"445\">unexplained changes in consciousness</strong>, confusion, or coma</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"468\" data-end=\"552\">\n<p data-start=\"470\" data-end=\"552\">Assess for <strong data-start=\"481\" data-end=\"507\">hepatic encephalopathy</strong> in patients with <strong data-start=\"525\" data-end=\"550\">chronic liver disease</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"553\" data-end=\"653\">\n<p data-start=\"555\" data-end=\"653\">Investigate <strong data-start=\"567\" data-end=\"591\">urea cycle disorders</strong>, especially in infants with vomiting, lethargy, or seizures</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"654\" data-end=\"732\">\n<p data-start=\"656\" data-end=\"732\">Monitor <strong data-start=\"664\" data-end=\"684\">liver transplant</strong> patients or those with severe hepatic failure</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"734\" data-end=\"862\">Ammonia is <strong data-start=\"745\" data-end=\"759\">neurotoxic</strong>, and elevated levels can cause <strong data-start=\"791\" data-end=\"809\">cerebral edema</strong>, especially in pediatric or critically ill patients.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Liver dysfunction (e.g., cirrhosis, hepatitis)</strong></td>\n<td>Impaired conversion of ammonia to urea; risk of hepatic encephalopathy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Urea cycle disorders</strong></td>\n<td>Congenital enzyme deficiencies (e.g., OTC deficiency) &rarr; toxic ammonia accumulation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reye's syndrome</strong> (children)</td>\n<td>Rare condition causing liver dysfunction and cerebral edema</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Portosystemic shunting</strong></td>\n<td>Bypass of liver metabolism in cirrhosis or congenital shunts leads to ammonia buildup</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>GI bleeding in liver disease</strong></td>\n<td>Digestion of blood increases nitrogen load &rarr; more ammonia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total parenteral nutrition (TPN)</strong></td>\n<td>Excess protein or amino acid infusion without adequate hepatic clearance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Certain drugs</strong> (e.g., valproate)</td>\n<td>Can inhibit ammonia metabolism, especially in predisposed individuals</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"734\" data-end=\"862\">&nbsp;</p>",
